Z||000005||Archie||07-28-2000||12:41 AM||||All of the questions on axles are answered in various places on my web site, but this thread would be a great place for me to explain it in detail in one place.<P>Please let me define one term before we start. The term "normal" SBC referrs to any and all SBC engines built from the mid 50's thru the current date, EXCEPT the LT1, LT4 & the LS1 engines. I will explain what makes those engines different later in this post.<P>Ok, let me try to start at the beginning. Using any "normal" SBC in a Fiero, my kit is designed to allow you to get the engine into the car with NO FRAME CUTTING and using the STOCK AXLES. To accomplish this the builder needs to use either an electric water pump or one of my custom belt drive W/P systems.<P>Now, just relax here and think this thru. Several people have voiced their opinions on my W/P drive system and the fact that it extends out to the outside of the frame rail. We should point out here that not one person who is critical of this system actually has one. You don't see my customers who are using the system on here complaining about it. To use this system you do have to trim a 3" Diameter hole in the sheet metal of the Fiero inner wheel well. I've been using this design for 11 years now and have sold more than 700 of them. NOT ONE PERSON, who installed it as instructed, has ever been stranded because of a failure of this design or it's execution. If you don't want to use an Electric Water pump, then the only alternative to my design is to cut a chunk out of the frame rail to use a regular style W/P drive. So what's a better idea.... should we trim a little sheet metal & have the drive on the outside of the frame rail or should we just cut away that frame rail all together? That's the reason I get worked up when the subject comes up. My method requires a little sheet metal trimming, the Zumwalt (or those who have copied his kits) style method requires that you remove a big chunk of the frame rail. I can't understand why anybody would dislike a system that keeps the Fiero frame intact.<P>Ok, now let's say you still have a problem with the W/P drive system, you still can use an Electric W/P. It will not violate the wheel well or the frame rail but in some climates it could have a hard time keeping up.  <P>I hope this explains this clearly enough. As far as the W/P system on a "normal" SBC engine, you have 3 choices.<P>1) My W/P drive design, which requires a little sheet metal trimming and has the drive outside the frame rail.<BR>2) A stock Chevy W/P drive belt system which requires that you remove a section of the frame rail.<BR>3) An electric W/P that fits within the engine compartment but sometimes has to labor to keep up.<P>You make the choice, but these are the facts.<P>Ok, now let's get back to the axle shaft question. Even though we were supposed to be talking axles all along, you need to know that the above W/P facts do have an impact on the Axle question.<P>I've already explained that my kit is designed to use the stock axles etc.<P>Now let's talk about the NON "normal" SBC engines & how axles relate to Water Pumps & frame modifications. In the early 90's Chevy started using different (or next generation) SBC engines in some of the performance production RWD cars. The LT1 & LT4 engines used the same basic engine block as the earlier SBC engines but GM took the distributer off of the intake manifold and put it on the front of the engine. This added a couple of inches to the assembled length of the engine. And on these 2 engines they put a new W/P on that was internally driven off of the camshaft. This pump didn't have a belt drive and it was unique to these engines only, it cannot be used on the older style engines. GM also made these engines with "Reverse Flow" cooling systems. On "Reverse Flow" cooling the water flowed down thru the engine cooling the heads first, all other SBC engines including the LS1 cool from the bottom up.<P>On the new LS1 the only carry-over design trait that effects us right now is the Bell-Housing bolt pattern. The block itself is about 1.5" shorter than all other SBC engines due to it's siamesed cylinders. As you know it is Aluminium and saves about 85 pounds over the LT1. However it's waterpump is some big old weird thing like you'd expect to see on a Ford. As far as a V-8 Fiero is concerned any advantage gained from haveing a 1.5" shorter engine block is more than lost by that big old waterpump sticking out there.<P>Ok, so how did all these changes effect the V-8 Fiero engine kits?<P>Well this is simple, as you may know, my kits for the "normal" SBC engines include a Harmonic Balancer Pulley. This HBP is used to keep the total length of the ASSEMBLED engine as short as possible so that it will fit BETWEEN the frame rails. This HBP cannot be used on the LT1, LT4 & LS1 applications, you must use the stock parts on these engines. When you take  into account all these changes GM made to these newer generation engines, you find that The TOTAL ASSEMBLED LENGTH of the LT1-4 engine is some 3.5" longer & the LS1 is about 2" longer than the same measurement on the "normal" engine.<P>So, NOW PAY ATTENTION HERE..... For the LT1, LT4 & LS1 engines, YOU MUST move them over to the left in the car about 2.3", YOU MUST modify the frame rails to accomodate that move & YOU MUST use custom length axles.<P>I hope this explains the facts clearly enough. I really gotta get some work done, I'll be out of town until Saturday night, then I'll try to answer questions you may have.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||07-28-2000||07:39 AM||||Archie,<P>Thank-you for a very well written explaination of your kit and the LS1, LT1 & LT4 motors. It's easy to see that you've done a tremendous amount of research and work. I have a couple of comments / questions:<P>For the LS1 kit, you mentioned that custom length axles are a "must" because you have to moving the transmission 2.3" toward the driver's side of the car. If this is the case, would you consider selling those axles along with your "regular" SBC kit? <P>I also realize that the motor mounts/brackets you use will not work if the motor is moved. Will you be considering manufacturing different mounts for the SBC conversion for those who would like to shift the motor?<P>Basically what I'm asking is: Are you going to offer two kits to install a first generation SBC into a Fiero. One basic that uses stock axles; another with custom axles that will allow the buyer to better center the motor? <P>Thanks,<BR>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000007||rprevo||07-30-2000||08:46 AM|||| That Ditributor mounting is a new one<BR>on me(With the LT1) Because the Numbers<BR>on my block and the metric piston rings<BR>all point back that my block is a 1993<BR>LT1 but my Distributor mounts in the old<BR>spot toward the back. The only thing<BR>I had to do was modify the carburated<BR>intake with 72 degree spotfaces in the<BR>center set of mounting holes.<P> Now I'm confused.||||reg||9
Z||000008||Archie||07-30-2000||09:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rprevo:<BR><B> That Ditributor mounting is a new one<BR>on me(With the LT1) Because the Numbers<BR>on my block and the metric piston rings<BR>all point back that my block is a 1993<BR>LT1 but my Distributor mounts in the old<BR>spot toward the back. The only thing<BR>I had to do was modify the carburated<BR>intake with 72 degree spotfaces in the<BR>center set of mounting holes.<P> Now I'm confused.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The LT1's that we are discussing look like this one (mounted in a '87 GT).<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/564.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/564.jpg</A> <P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000009||rprevo||07-30-2000||10:19 AM|||| So If you remove all of that upper<BR>Fuel injected stuff such as that fancy<BR>intake and fuel runner what is really<BR>different about that block?<BR>Is all the true changes strictly bolt on<BR>chages?<BR>Just curious.<BR> Ray ||||reg||14
Z||000010||Archie||07-30-2000||10:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>Archie,<P> would you consider selling those axles along with your "regular" SBC kit? <P>Basically what I'm asking is: Are you going to offer two kits to install a first generation SBC into a Fiero. One basic that uses stock axles; another with custom axles that will allow the buyer to better center the motor? <P>Thanks,<BR>Roy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, I'm going to try to make this answer a little shorter than some of the others I've written lately.<P>Yes, I can sell the custom length axles with my regular kits, but I'm not planning on doing a 2nd kit just for centering the engine.<P>If you are considering moving the engine to the left just to center the weight or something like that then I think that is not such a great idea. Several years ago a SCCA friend & I used his scales to determine what would be the weight change by moving the SBC 2-3/8" to the left in the chassis. While I cannot remember the exact weigh for each corner of that car, I do remember that moving the SBC to the left only transferred 8 pounds toward the left side of the car. (The left corner weight gained 8 pounds and the right lost 8 pounds...making a 16 pound difference)<P>You also need to remember that, on a stick shift car, moving the drive line to the left in the car requires you to knotch the left side frame rail. So you would have the additional cost of the axles and frame modifications only for an 8 pound change.<P>If you are doing an LT1, LT4 or LS1 you HAVE to move the engine over & thus you HAVE to bite down on the additional expense. On a "normal" SBC swap you don't HAVE to do that modification as it would return very little benifit for the investment.<P>If you are considering moving the engine over to accommodate a normal style Chevy W/P drive then consider this. To move the engine to the left to clear the Chevy style W/P drive you are committing yourself to modifying the LEFT side frame rail AND purchasing axles. If you leave the engine where it is you can use the Chevy W/P drive by modifying the RIGHT side frame rail PLUS you wouldn't have to buy the axles. So either way you are going to have to modify ONE frame rail, why not save the expense of the axles and modify the right side frame rail?<P>Heck, I'd be happy to sell you the additional parts you'd need, but only after making sure you are totally informed.<P>Just the facts.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000011||squid||07-30-2000||11:06 AM||||Archie, do you have time to develop a variation of your "normal" sbc kit for the 4.3L v-6? I bought a partial kit from you and I have been working on it for awhile (as spare time permits--I'm in no hurry to yank a good engine either). A LOT of interest has been expressed with using this engine. It could satisfy those who dont want to cut or use an electric pump. Since the block is 4 inches shorter, a flat plate mounted horizontally between your front "V" mount and the stock rubber mount would seem to be all you would need to add to your v8 kit. sliding the transaxle left, H.D. radiator, special grooved harmonic damper, and flipping the water pump may not even be necessary. Typical torque output for mildly hopped up 4.3's is 260 ft/lbs. The pre-'92 engines vibrate more but you'd be keeping the front rubber mount and there would be a little less weight in the rear. They were in '85-up astrovans and '88-up s-10's. Not much R&D for an increase in sales. There would always be the guys who have to have a v8 and v6 guys who want to upgrade to a v8 later($$$$$$$). My 2 cents.      ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||07-31-2000||12:00 PM||||I must "second" the 4.3 request. Always thought it would be an interesting swap. <BR>Archie, I understand that you have tons to do, but any input or advice would be much appreciated. <BR>Need a beta tester for a new kit design?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Might just open up a whole new niche.<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||07-31-2000||07:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by squid:<BR><B>Archie, do you have time to develop a variation of your "normal" sbc kit for the 4.3L v-6? <P> My 2 cents.      </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You guys are right, I really should have a proper kit setup for the 4.3.<P>I've thought about it many times over the years. The main reason I have not done one myself is that when I have a car to convert, I go out and shop for an engine. I always prefer to buy new engines and when pricing engines you find many times that you can purchase a new SBC engine cheaper than a new 4.3. I know it's no excuse but when I can have a V-8 for the same price or less than a 4.3 I've always bought the V-8. You remember the advertizing phrase "I could'a had a V-8 for the price of a V-6" I think that's how it went.  :-)<P>Anyway my 92 Chevy W/T has 160K on it's 4.3 and it still runs pretty good. So now might be a good time to stick a V-8 in it and I can use the old 4.3 to stick into a Fiero.<P>I've got 6 customer cars in the shop right now to finish then I have to build a Racing Fino for the Kit Car Illustrated Run & Gun event for the end of September. I also sold my White IMSA car last week, so I need to get my F-40 on the road for a daily driver while I finish the brakes and interior on my Finale. Then I can jump all over that pick-up truck.<P>Should be a piece of cake.<P>Archie||||reg||1
